findings.program:
struct Holder[T]:
    borrowed: str
    marker: T
    fn drop(mut self):
        println(self.borrowed)

fn text() -> String:
    return "still live"

fn hold(s: str) -> Holder[i32]:
    return Holder(s, 0)

fn width(h: Holder[i32]) -> int:
    return h.borrowed.char_count()

fn main():
    println(width(hold(text().as_str())))

Holder[i32] is a view, so it is passed by value: `hold(...)` goes through lower_operand and its temporary H is registered before the String temporary S made for `text()`. At the end of the statement the drops are S, then H. Per [EXP-4] they should be H, then S. Expected: E3060 refusing a valid program, or, if the check misses it, Holder.drop printing a freed String. The same order comes from `hold(text().as_str())` as a bare expression statement (Stmt::Expr temporary) or as a match scrutinee.

findings.evidence:
When an interface's associated-type bounds are collected:
```
let positional = args.iter().any(|arg| !matches!(arg, ast::GenericArg::Assoc { .. }));
let instance = match positional {
    false => self.resolve_name(segment.name),
    true => match self.resolve_interface_use(bound) { ... },
};
bounds.push(instance);
```
For `type Scalar: Add` or `type Scalar: Add[Output = Scalar]`, no argument is positional, so the bound stored is the generic interface itself, `std.core.Add`, not an instance. A type parameter's bound goes through the arm at 3505 ("a generic interface named bare takes its defaults, with Self the parameter: T: Add is Add[T]"), and `T: Add[Output = T]` goes through bound_interface, so it is `Add[T]`. assoc_bounds_of hands the origin `std.core.Add` to the hidden parameter S.Scalar unchanged (close_bounds adds nothing).
This breaks two things.
(a) Implementation check (9019-9029): `bound_for_owner(bound, &[], ty)` returns an origin unchanged because open_interface_origin has no entry for it. Then `implements(f64, std.core.Add)` is false: `implemented` holds (f64, the Add[f64] instance) and never the origin, and range_operator_implements returns None for a non-range f64 with no args. So `type Scalar = f64` gets E2040 "`Box2`'s `Scalar` is `f64`, which does not implement `Add`".
(b) Generic body: synth_bound_operator finds `std.core.Add` in the bounds, and is_operator_bound accepts it. But the origin's `add` takes `rhs: Rhs`, a raw `TyKind::Param{index 0, name Rhs}`, and `rhs.ty == param` fails for an S.Scalar operand. So `a + b` is E2040 "`S.Scalar`'s bounds do not provide a matching `Add` operator". The same `a + b` on `T: Add[Output = T]` is accepted. Per [IFC-4], T.Name has what its declared bounds provide, and per Part IV section 8 a bare generic interface takes its defaults with Self the bounded type. Existing tests only use non-generic or positional bounds (`type Member: Tag[T]`, `type Item: Copy`), so none covers this.

findings.program:
fn sum2[T: Add[Output = T]](a: T, b: T) -> T.Output:
    return a + b

fn first[I: Iterator[Item = int]](owned it: I) -> I.Item:
    return it.next().unwrap_or(0)

fn bump[I: Iterator[Item = int]](x: I.Item) -> int:
    return x + 1

Expected: all three accepted (Output is T, Item is int, as the bounds say). Actual: sum2 and first give a type mismatch (`T` / `int` found where the hidden `T.Output` / `I.Item` is expected), and bump gives E2040 (I.Item's bounds provide no Add).

fn show_all[I: Iterator](owned it: I) where I.Item: Display:
    for x in it:
        println(f"{x}")

fn main():
    show_all([1, 2, 3].into_iter())

Expected: accepted (the clause gives I.Item Display). Actual: E2040 at the f-string hole ("requires `Display` or `Debug` on `I.Item`"), with nothing pointing at the ignored clause. Also, a body that does not use the bound accepts calls whose item type does not meet the clause.

open class Base:
    items: Array[int]

    fn init(mut self):
        self.items = [1, 2, 3]

    fn pick[T](self, tag: T) -> Span[int]:
        return self.items[..]

class Derived(Base):
    fn init(mut self):
        super.init()

fn main():
    d = Derived()
    v = d.pick(7)
    println(v)

Expected: compiles and prints [1, 2, 3]. Likely result: the emitted call passes `&d` (struct em_obj_Derived**) to the instance's `struct em_obj_Base**` self parameter with no cast. That is a C warning on clang and MSVC and an error on GCC 14+, and the callee does a strict-aliasing-violating read. Before D-460 the same program got a typed `ref Base` to a retained temporary (still E3060 if `v` outlived the statement).

open class Base:
    items: Array[int]

    fn init(mut self):
        self.items = [1, 2]

    fn view(self) -> Span[int]:
        return self.items[..]

class Derived(Base):
    fn init(mut self):
        super.init()

    fn both(self) -> Span[int]:
        return self.view()      # result borrows self ([LT-1] rule 1)

fn main():
    d = Derived()
    println(d.both())

Expected: accepted, prints [1, 2]. Likely result: E3060 at `self.view()` because the view borrows the upcast temporary of `*self`, as D-460 described for `d.view()` before the fix. The same applies to `r = ref d; v = r.view(); println(v)`.

fn main():
    let a = Arena.with_capacity(64)
    let r = a.alloc(())   # Arena alloc of the unit value
    println("ok")
# Generated C contains `(void*)ember_arena_alloc_copy((a)->state, 0, 1, &0)`; the C compiler rejects it (lvalue required as unary '&' operand). The same happens with FixedArena.alloc(()) and ScopedArena.alloc(()). Nothing is wrong at the Ember level.

